Hi all,

I fitted a non-spatial single-season occupancy models to opportunistic citizen-science data. I only include the sites/grid cells that at least have one observation of any species and generate non-detection within these grid cells. Grid cells with no observation records are not included because in my understanding, including the cells will underestimates detection probability (lots of grid cells will contain NAs across replicates).
As a result, the grid are not evenly distributed, largely reflecting observer access and activity rather a systematic spatial design. I did try to run spatial model with the same MCMC settings but the running time is 2 times slower than the non-spatial model, and the spatial parameter does not converge really well (low ESS, high rhat) and produce very high standard deviation.

My question is that is there any rule of thumb on when to use spatial vs non-spatial model? My concern is I have about 20 species to analyse (and will increase over time) and if I go for the spatial model, then I will need to increase iteration, investigate further on the prior distributions and tuning parameter (Metropolis), and this will take a longer time. I am not sure if adding spatial autocorrelation to the occupancy component is appropriate given the unstructured data.

Best

